{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And behold, a na'ar weeping</b> — his voice was heard like that of a na'ar (youth), in the language of Rabbi Shelomo (Rashi). But they already rejected this and said: if so, you have made our master Moses a ba'al mum (one with a blemish) (Sotah 12a; Shemot Rabbah 1:28). And furthermore, what reason is there for Scripture to mention the thickness of his voice? And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra) said (on Exodus 2:6) that his limbs were large like those of a na'ar. Perhaps Scripture mentioned this for his beauty, for on account of his goodness and beauty she had compassion on him, for she recognized in him that he was born recently and his limbs were fine and well-formed as though he were a <b>na'ar</b>. And it is more correct to interpret that he was <b>weeping</b> like a na'ar in vigor and alacrity, and therefore she had compassion on him. And the Aggadah of <b>na'ar bokheh</b>: he was a child but his behavior was like that of a na'ar; Gabriel came and struck Moses so that he would cry and she would be filled with compassion for him (Shemot Rabbah 1:28).<br><b>And in my opinion</b> there is no need for any of this, for the child from the day he is born may be called a na'ar, as it says, \"What shall we do for the na'ar who is born?\" (Judges 13:8), and likewise, \"And David sought Elohim on behalf of the na'ar\" (II Samuel 12:16). And he may be called a yeled even when he is grown, as is said regarding Ishmael (Genesis 21:15), and likewise, \"And the na'ar was a na'ar\" (I Samuel 1:24), meaning that he was a small child of twenty-four months. For when he was weaned from milk she immediately brought him up, as it is written (ibid. vv. 23–24), \"And she nursed her son until she weaned him, and she brought him up with her when she had weaned him.\"" "<b>And she said, 'From the Hebrew women is this one'</b> — she set her mind to the matter as it actually was, for she said: in order to save him, or perhaps so that they would not be seen at the death of the child, they placed him there — but why would an Egyptian man do such a thing? And there are those who say that she saw he was circumcised (Sotah 12a; Shemot Rabbah 1:29). If so, she removed his garments and examined him. And there is no need for this."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes I Samuel 1:23–24 as two consecutive phrases from adjacent verses; he cites them together as 'שם ש״כ ש״ד,' meaning both verse 23 and 24 at that location."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:13.516464610Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}